# mm.audio.es8311

The Everest Semiconductor ES8311 audio codec over portable mm.mcu I2C and I2S:
its DAC as an mm.audio Out and its ADC as an mm.audio In. It contains no vendor
header and no board pin number: a board's provider supplies the wiring. It sits
where mm.imu.qmi8658 sits for the IMU, because the ES8311 is a chip with
registers worth writing once.

The chip is three classes. Codec is the chip itself -- the control bus, the
reset, the clocks, and the one I2S link both directions share -- and is not an
audio device. Output and Input are, and each holds a Codec. A board that plays
and records through one ES8311 constructs one Codec and one of each and
registers them as its Speaker and its Microphone. A board whose link has no
receive line registers the Output and, as docs/modules-audio.mdy asks of a
board without a microphone, an In of its own whose every call answers
Unsupported; an Input there would answer BadArgument, which says the wiring is
wrong rather than that the board has no microphone.
Two classes rather than one because a class cannot be an In and an Out at once
under one level of dispatch: their calls share names and not meanings.

The codec is identified by its two chip identification registers, 0x83 and
0x11 at 0xfd and 0xfe, and a part that answers otherwise is Unsupported. It is
controlled at 0x18, or 0x19 with CE high. The first initialize of either
direction resets it and powers up what both share; each direction then powers
up its own converter and leaves its serial port muted, the DAC's at 0x09 and
the ADC's at 0x0a. The link runs as the platform's slave with the codec's
master clock taken from the bit clock, multiplied to 256 times the rate, so a
board needs no MCLK line. Its one word clock means one rate for both
directions: a configure at another rate moves the link only while neither
direction is started, and a direction left at the old rate configures again.
start unmutes a port and stop mutes it before discarding the transport's
buffer, while the clocks keep running.

Output keeps the Out contract: service offers its pending run first and reads
the Stream only when nothing is pending, each sample goes to both slots, and
transmitter silence is reported as underrun. Input keeps the In contract:
service takes no more than the Stream has room for, so it never holds a sample
it cannot write, the analog microphone's sample is the input_slot word, and
receiver drops are reported as overrun. The microphone's PGA gain is the
board's, zero to ten steps of three decibels. Slots are sixteen or thirty-two
bits wide.

The register values follow the datasheet's map as Espressif's driver programs
it for this mode. The first version of this driver, on the audio-work branch,
addressed the part at 0x06, programmed the ADC's serial port 0x0a for
playback, and never enabled the clock manager, the analog section, or the DAC;
each is corrected here, and the ADC it did not drive is now an Input. None of
this is qualified on hardware until a board with the codec plays and records
through it.
